    "title": "Millions of Android users qualify for part of a $135 million settlement: What to know",
    "text": "NewsGoogle was accused of violating Android users' privacy. (Photo Illustration by Omar Marques/SOPA Images/LightRocket via Getty Images)SOPA Images/LightRocket via GettSome Android mobile device users may be eligible for a portion of a $135 million class-action settlement.Google has agreed to a $135 million settlement to resolve claims it used cellular data on Android devices to transfer information to Google without user permission, TopClassAction.com reported. Google could have opted to make the transfers only when the device was connected to WiFi but instead chose to use cellular data. The transfers allegedly occurred in the background, even when devices were not in use.Google has not admitted wrongdoing.The settlement covers customers with mobile devices who used Android operating systems to access the internet through cellular data networks since Nov. 12, 2017.Customers who qualify for the settlement can receive a cash payment but the exact amount will depend on the number of people who participate in the suit. According to the settlement website, that could be as many as 100 million class members.No action is required to benefit from the settlement and class members who don’t exclude themselves will automatically receive benefits. Google is also required to update its terms of service, its help center pages and set up screens used by customers.The deadline to exclude yourself or object to the class action is May 29. The final hearing on the settlement is June 23. Customers who aren’t sure if they are included in the settlement can call the administrator at 1-844-655-4255.",
